---
title: Stuart rotates a key with an SMT Beacon, Satoshi verifies
---

%% Use case: Stuart and Satoshi are peers in a p2p, Signal-like messaging channel.
%% Each peer is identified by a did:btcr2 DID. Satoshi trusts Stuart's keys at version N.
%% The aggregation steps follow the RECOMMENDED MuSig2 example of the specification.

sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor Stu as Stuart (Aggregation Participant)
    participant Others as Other Aggregation Participants
    participant AS as Aggregation Service
    participant BTC as Bitcoin blockchain ⛓
    participant CAS as CAS / IPFS 📦 (optional)
    actor Sat as Satoshi (channel peer)
    participant Res as Satoshi's resolver

    rect rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.08)
    Note over Stu,AS: 0. Setup, one time: join an SMT Aggregation Cohort
    Stu->>AS: Enroll: didIndex = SHA256(did), Schnorr public key
    AS->>Stu: n-of-n P2TR Beacon Address and the n public keys
    Note over Stu,AS: Each Beacon Signal spends a UTXO of the n-of-n Beacon Address.<br/>The specification does not say which party funds it.
    Stu->>Stu: Compute the address again. Make sure that it contains Stuart's key.
    Stu->>BTC: Update the DID to add an SMTBeacon service<br/>(announced through the Singleton Beacon)
    end

    rect rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.08)
    Note over Stu: 1. Construct the update
    Stu->>Stu: Resolve own DID (minConf 6) → versionId N
    Stu->>Stu: Construct and sign the BTCR2 Update with the new key material<br/>(targetVersionId N + 1), then verify the proof
    Stu->>Stu: updateId = SHA256(JCS(BTCR2 Signed Update))<br/>new nonce for this Beacon Signal<br/>leaf value = hash(hash(nonce) + updateId)
    end

    rect rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.08)
    Note over Stu,BTC: 2. Aggregate and announce (privacy: no did and no update go to the service)
    AS->>Stu: Update opportunity
    AS->>Others: Update opportunity
    par Every participant responds
        Stu->>AS: didIndex, leaf value, MuSig2 nonce
    and
        Others->>AS: didIndex, leaf value (update or no update), MuSig2 nonce
    end
    AS->>AS: Build the optimized SMT from all indexes → root.<br/>Unsigned Beacon Signal with OP_RETURN root. Aggregate the nonces.
    AS->>Stu: SMT Proof { id: root, collapsed, hashes },<br/>Unsigned Beacon Signal, aggregated nonce
    AS->>Others: SMT Proofs, Unsigned Beacon Signal, aggregated nonce
    Stu->>Stu: SMT Proof Verification with nonce and updateId<br/>against the root in the Unsigned Beacon Signal
    Stu->>AS: Partial signature
    Others->>AS: Partial signatures
    AS->>BTC: Aggregate the partial signatures, broadcast the Beacon Signal
    BTC-->>Stu: minConf (6) confirmations
    Stu->>Stu: Keep the update, the nonce, and the SMT Proof for the life of the DID
    Note over Stu,AS: In each later round, Stuart responds with no update:<br/>leaf value = hash(hash(new nonce)). Stuart keeps that nonce and SMT Proof too.
    end

    rect rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.08)
    Note over Stu,Sat: 3. Distribute the update data
    opt CAS distribution (the SMT Proofs never go to CAS)
        Stu->>CAS: Publish the BTCR2 Signed Update
    end
    Stu->>Sat: Channel message signed with the new key, did, and Sidecar Data:<br/>updates, smtProofs (one for each Beacon Signal of the SMT Beacon)
    end

    rect rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.08)
    Note over Sat,Res: 4. Satoshi verifies
    Sat->>Res: resolve(did, { sidecar })
    Res->>BTC: Find Beacon Signals of the Singleton and SMT Beacon Addresses
    BTC-->>Res: Beacon Signals (Signal Bytes = root), block metadata
    Res->>Res: Process SMT Beacon: smt_proof = smtProofs[root],<br/>SMT Proof Verification from hash(did) → root
    alt smt_proof has updateId
        Res->>Res: Get the update from the Sidecar Data (else from CAS by updateId)
        Res->>Res: Process Next Update: check the hashes and the proof, apply the patch
    else smt_proof has no updateId
        Res->>Res: No update for Stuart's DID in this Beacon Signal
    end
    Res-->>Sat: didDocument (version N + 1) and didDocumentMetadata
    Sat->>Sat: Check: not deactivated, versionId ≥ N, new key is in authentication.<br/>Verify the channel message with the new key. Trust the new key.
    end
